  "meta": {
    "title": "Molt Tide: Copepod Exuviae Beneath the Chukchi Pack Ice",
    "date": "2026-11-27",
    "lesson": "Under Arctic fast ice, millions of copepods (Calanus, Metridia) molt and migrate daily, shedding chitinous exoskeletons that sink slowly through frigid, stratified water. This 'molt tide' is a steady, cold-adapted analog to a marine-snow fall: chitinolytic bacteria like Vibrio and Pseudoalteromonas-type psychrophiles sense and swim toward the dissolving shells (chemotaxis up a mucilage gradient), carve the chitin with chitobiase, and divide slowly in near-freezing, nutrient-rich but light-starved water. Pennate ice diatoms glide on the underside of the pack, feeding a thin rain of exudate carbon, while grazing protists move sluggishly in the cold (Q10 penalty) and phage pressure persists but bursts slowly. The lesson: cold does not mean lifeless -- it means everything, growth, grazing, viral turnover, runs on a slower clock, while the chitin supply itself stays rich because molting never stops.",
    "citation": "Arctic under-ice biogeochemistry and copepod molt flux studies, Chukchi/Beaufort fast ice regions (e.g. ICESCAPE, SUBICE campaigns).",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Arctic land-fast sea ice over the Chukchi shelf; copepod diel vertical migration and molting beneath the ice; psychrophilic chitinolytic bacterial colonization of sinking exoskeleton particles."
  },
